<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tanguts</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tangut people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tanguts were a people of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, speaking a Tibeto-Burman language, who lived in the Ordos and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Gansu corridor</a>. Under the Li clan they founded the Western Xia state, whose ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-yuanhao">Li Yuanhao</a> declared himself emperor in 1038 and introduced a Tangut script in 1036. Their state was destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in 1227, and thousands of Tangut texts were later recovered at Khara-Khoto.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tanguts.jpg" alt="Tanguts" width="320" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E8%A5%BF%E5%A4%8F%E7%94%B7%E4%BE%9B%E9%A4%8A%E4%BA%BA%E5%83%8F.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The early Tanguts lived on the steppes and mountains of south-east Qinghai and north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, regions that correspond to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amdo">Amdo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kham">Kham</a> on the Tibetan plateau. They first lived under Tuyuhun authority and later submitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>. Their leader Tuoba Chici, probably of mixed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> and Qiang heritage, was given the title Captain General of Western-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rong-people">Rong</a> and the surname Li. In the early 8th century pressure from the Tibetan Empire forced them north to the eastern Ordos. By the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan-rebellion">An Lushan Rebellion</a> (755-763) they were the predominant local power in what is now eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>, Ningxia and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 881 the Tanguts helped the Tang suppress the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huang-chao">Huang Chao</a> rebellion, and the general Li Sigong received the prefectures of Xia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and Yin as hereditary titles. After the Tang collapsed they expanded south-west. In 1002 they conquered Ling Prefecture and set up a first capital at Xiping. By 1036 they had annexed the Guiyi Circuit and the Ganzhou Uyghur Kingdom and had conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xining">Xining</a>. The state of Western Xia was proclaimed in 1038.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-yuanhao">Li Yuanhao</a>, who took the throne as Emperor Jingzong, sought to set his state apart from Song China. He ordered an official Tangut script, required traditional dress and short or shaved hair for men, and took the Tangut surname Weiming in place of Li and Zhao. His capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yinchuan">Xingqing</a>, modern Yinchuan. Genghis Khan attacked Western Xia in six campaigns between 1205 and 1227 and died in its territory during the last. In 1227 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> overran the capital, burnt its buildings and records, killed the last emperor and massacred tens of thousands of civilians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Tangut society was divided into the Red Faced, who were commoners, and the Black Headed, an elite priestly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> named for the black caps of Root West shamans. According to myth the Black Headed descended from a heavenly white crane and the Red Faced from a monkey. The Tanguts were primarily Buddhists. The Chinese Buddhist canon was translated into Tangut over about fifty years and published around 1090 in about 3,700 fascicles. The state supervised teachers, required government approval for monks and allowed women to serve as nuns, though only if widowed or unmarried.</p><p>The Tangut language, also called Fan, is a tonal Tibeto-Burman language, and later research places it in the Gyalrongic branch of Qiangic. Both it and its script are extinct. Tangut families joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> after 1227, and under the Yuan the Tanguts were counted as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semu">Semu</a>. Small communities survived in Anhui and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>, and Tanguts in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> kept their language until at least the 16th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tangut people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Tanguts</b>, Dangxiang to the Chinese and Minyak to the Tibetans, came from the highlands of the Qinghai-Sichuan-Tibet region and were held to descend from the Western Qiang.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-xia">Western Xia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…office, taxes and law: the Mongols; the semu, “various sorts”, peoples of Central and West Asia such as Uyghurs, <b>Tanguts</b> and Muslims; the Han ren, subjects of the former Jin state in the north; and the nanren, “southerners”, subjects of the Song and by far the most numerous.</blockquote><a href="/societies/yuan-china">Social Hierarchy of Yuan China</a></li><li><blockquote>He allied with the Song Dynasty, which gave him titles and subsidies, and defeated <b>Tangut</b> attacks from Western Xia in the 1030s.</blockquote><a href="/state/tsongkha">Tsongkha</a></li></ul></section>
